00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.300 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  dear BuzzFeed when I was four I just  thought I was like any other boy as I  grew a little older I started realizing  I was different for a while I believed  that I was just a late developer by the  time I realized this wasn't going to  happen I was seven or eight at this age  I did everything I could to fit in and  be seen as one of the boys I were all  boys clothes was happy when people use  male pronouns for me went out in public  and I got my hair cut short a  combination of secondary school age 11  and puberty forced me into thinking I  had to accept the way I was physically  and I tried to fit in as female during  my early teens I could never find  clothes I liked how uncomfortable on  anything I wore and disliked my hair  being long when I was 16 I watched a  documentary on channel 4 about a trans  guy the way he was feeling and how he  had felt flicked a switch and I suddenly  had a name for how I was feeling and  realized that I could actually live as  who I felt I was between my first and  second year at college I came out as  transgender and went back as Jamie for  my a-levels I told my mum first who in  turn told my dad and my brother I then  told my group of friends and finally my  girlfriend who was my best friend at the  time I'm so grateful that they were all  amazingly supportive and over time this  increased as they understood more and  more my mum and I went to speak to my GP  about being referred to a gender  identity clinic so that I could pursue  hormones and eventually surgery the day  I started testosterone was incredibly  exciting my dad picked up my  prescription for me and it was sitting  waiting for me when I got home from  college  so home ensure this as the weeks and  months went by I noticed more and more  changes voice drop in the last month my  muscles are more defined and they are  bigger it's my voice  it's breaking as well I was all that  peach fuzz being  all these changes made me feel so much  more comfortable myself and as grateful  and happy as I was to build hormones I  knew I needed top surgery six months  later I had the surgery the recovery was  uncomfortable and frustrating  it took a couple months for me to start  properly seeing the full effects and  feel the benefits of having a flat chest  [Music]  the only thing that's changing this much  scars are continuing to fade I'm really  happy with whether  we seem to just keep getting lighter  which is pretty awesome now a little  over three years later I still feel  grateful for my chest all the time  the positive impact that is out of my  life is indescribable and that stage in  my life now where I feel content and so  much more comfortable with myself and  with my body my self-esteem and  confidence has increased massively and I  feel a sense of freedom and acceptance  with who I am now something that made  everything easier in the beginning was  having a support network I was  incredibly fortunate to have strong  support from my friends and family if I  were to advise anyone who is just  starting out or about to start their  transition I would say that patience  will be your best friend support is  crucial from wherever you can get it and  concentrate on the little things and  what you can change now  this is a hard process that at times  will feel impossible and impossibly so  but you will get there one day  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

